. **Fold (-1)**: This would be extremely weak given my strong hand. A-J is well above average and has good equity against most hands. Folding would be a waste of a premium starting hand.\n\n2. **Check (0)**: This appears to be the big blind position since I can check. While checking is safe and allows me to see the flop for free, it gives up the initiative and doesn't build the pot when I likely have the best hand pre-flop.\n\n3. **Raise (200+)**: A standard raise would be around 3x the big blind (likely 200-300 chips based on the valid actions). This is a strong play with A-J as it:\n   - Builds the pot when I likely have equity advantage\n   - Takes initiative in the hand\n   - May fold out weaker hands or get value from worse aces/pairs\n   - Defines my opponent's hand strength based on their response\n\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Going all-in with 19,950 chips pre-flop would be extremely aggressive and likely unnecessary. While A-J is strong, it's not strong enough to risk my entire stack before seeing any community cards in a 20-hand match.\n\nGiven that I'm down 500 chips cumulatively and this is only hand 5 of 20, I have plenty of time to recover. A standard raise is the optimal play here to extract value and maintain proper aggression with a premium hand.\n\nReason:\nA-J offsuit is a premium starting hand in heads-up play with strong equity against most opponent ranges. A standard raise of 200 chips (assuming 2-3x big blind) allows me to build the pot with a likely advantage, take control of the hand, and gather information about my opponent's holding while risking a reasonable amount. This balanced approach is superior to the passivity of checking or the recklessness of going all-in.\n\nAnswer: 200"}
